{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-126738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=(1-t)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,-1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $P(2,-1,-1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-j-k \\\\ (1-t)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k & =2\\,i-j-k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-t=2 \\\\ -{t}^{2}=-1 \\\\ 2t+1=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-1,-1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(-1).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i-2t\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(-1) & =-i-2(-1)\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-1,-1)$ is $-i+2\\,j+2\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-126738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\,i+j-k"] ["math" "-i+2\\,j+2\\,k"] ["math" "2\\,i+2\\,j-k"] ["math" "2\\,i+j+2\\,k"] ["math" "i-2\\,j-2\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-i+2\\,j+2\\,k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,i+{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,i+{sec}^{2}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  is  $\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-75198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k"] ["math" "2\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j+\\,k"] ["math" "2\\,i-2\\,j+\\,k"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j+\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t+\\frac{1}{t},\\,t-\\frac{1}{t}⟩$ at the point where $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=1$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(1)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}},\\,1+\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-1,\\,1+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=1$  is  $⟨0,\\,2⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-75197" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75197" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2,\\,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,\\,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,\\,-2⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,\\,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75197" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,\\,2⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)],\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-(-1)),\\,2(1+0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $  is  $⟨4,\\,2⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2,\\,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,\\,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,\\,-2⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,\\,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,\\,4⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨4,\\,2⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=(3t+2)\\,i+(6t+1)\\,j+(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k$ at the point $P(2,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+j+k \\\\ (3t+2)\\,i+(6t+1)\\,j+(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k & =2\\,i+j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3t+2=2 \\\\ 6t+1=1 \\\\ 1-4{t}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8(0)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,1,1)$ is $3\\,i+6\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,i+\\,j"] ["math" "2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k"] ["math" "3\\,i+6\\,j"] ["math" "3\\,i-8\\,k"] ["math" "6\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\,i+6\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j+(t-1)\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=2,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=2$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(2)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r(2)+u{r}^{′}(2),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $r(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =({2}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}}\\,j+(2-1)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2) & =2(2)\\,i+\\frac{(2)}{\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=2$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-33457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+2\\sqrt{5}\\,j+2\\,k)"] ["math" "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+2\\,k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨0,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve  $r(t)=\\sqrt{1+t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+(3t-1)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,4,8),$  valid for all real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{0}$ corresponds to the value of $t$ at the point $P.$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k \\\\ \\sqrt{1+t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+(3t-1)\\,k & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+t}=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-5=4 \\\\ 3t-1=8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=3.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(3).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1+t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+t}}\\,i+2t\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+(3)}}\\,i+2(3)\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,i+2\\cdot 3\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(2,4,8)$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-32920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k)"] ["math" "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+3\\,j-3\\,k)"] ["math" "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k)"] ["math" "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i-6\\,j-3\\,k)"] ["math" "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(i+6\\,j+6\\,k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-32920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{sin}^{2}t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+u{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j+{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j-\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})k \\\\  & =\\,i-\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(-\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(2\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i+2\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)"] ["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(i-\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+3t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & ={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3(\\pi )\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+3\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{(\\pi )}\\,i+3\\,j+\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2(\\pi )k \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\pi $ is \n\n$$\nR(u)={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-33491" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33491" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j)"] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j+2\\,k)"] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+\\,j)"] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+j+2\\,k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33491" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j]\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=\\pm 1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)=1\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j=\\,i+j, \\\\ r(-1)=(-1)\\,i+\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\,j=-\\,i-j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-75521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,-1)"] ["math" "(-1,0)"] ["math" "(1,1)"] ["text" "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-75521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨1-{t}^{2},3t+4⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $⟨1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-2t,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1(-2t)+1(3) & =0 \\\\ -2t+3 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨1-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},3(\\frac{3}{2})+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\frac{9}{4},\\frac{9}{2}+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-126896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2})"] ["math" "(\\frac{11}{2},\\frac{5}{4})"] ["math" "(\\frac{11}{2},\\frac{5}{8})"] ["math" "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{11}{4})"] ["math" "(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{11}{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{5}\\,,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{5})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 10{t}^{4}+(-5)\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 10{t}^{4}-10t & =0 \\\\ 10t({t}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{5}\\,,\\,(1)^{2}-2⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2\\,,\\,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-164833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2\\,,\\,-1)"] ["math" "(64,2)"] ["math" "(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})"] ["text" "$(2,-1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$"] ["text" "$(64,2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2\\,,\\,-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j]\\cdot (i+t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot t & =0 \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(4+{t}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since $4+{t}^{2}>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=0\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})\\,j=j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,1)"] ["math" "(2,3)"] ["text" "$(0,1)$ and $(2,3)$"] ["text" "$(0,1)$, $(2,2)$, and $(-2,2)$"] ["text" "$(2,3)$ and $(-2,3)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-8)\\,i-4t\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-8)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [({t}^{2}-8)\\,i-4t\\,j]\\cdot (2t\\,i-4\\,j) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-8)\\cdot 2t+(-4t)\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{3}-16t+16t & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=({0}^{2}-8)\\,i-4(0)\\,j=-8i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-8,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-164836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-8,0)"] ["math" "(9,12)"] ["text" "$(-4,8)$ and $(9,12)$"] ["text" "$(-7,-4)$ and $(-4,-8)$"] ["text" "$(-8,0)$ and $(-7,-4)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-164836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-8,0)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k & =c(i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 2+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ 1+{t}^{2}=c(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the equation $1+{t}^{2}=c(2t).$\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =i+(2+{1}^{3})\\,j+(1+{1}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(1,3,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,2,1)"] ["math" "(1,3,2)"] ["math" "(2,10,5)"] ["text" "$(0,2,1)$ and $(-1,1,2)$"] ["text" "$(1,3,2)$ and $(-1,3,2)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,3,2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =c(2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =2c{e}^{2t}\\,i+c(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}=2c{e}^{2t} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2}=c(2t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting $c=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2t+1) \\\\ {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the point corresponding to $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & ={e}^{0}\\,i+({0}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,\\frac{1}{2})"] ["math" "(e,\\frac{5}{4})"] ["math" "({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})"] ["text" "$(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})$"] ["text" "$(e,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $({e}^{-2},\\frac{1}{2})$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,\\frac{1}{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨4t-{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩,$ find all points at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨2,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =c⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4-2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨2c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4-2t=2c \\\\ 3{t}^{2}=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2-t.$ Substituting in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & =2-t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+t-2 & =0 \\\\ (3t-2)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨2,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=-1,\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨4(-1)-(-1)^{2},(-1)^{3}⟩=⟨-5,-1⟩ \\\\ r(\\frac{2}{3}) & =⟨4(\\frac{2}{3})-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2},{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}⟩=⟨\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨2,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-126901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-5,-1)$ and $(0,0)$"] ["text" "$(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27})$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27})$"] ["text" "$(3,1)$ and $(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{5}{9})$"] ["text" "$(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{5}{9})$  and $(0,0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-126901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27})$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t}\\,,\\,{t}^{3}+5\\,,\\,2t+2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,{t}^{3}+5,\\,2t+2⟩ & =c\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,{t}^{3}+5,\\,2t+2⟩ & =⟨\\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3c{t}^{2},\\,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t}=\\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ {t}^{3}+5=3c{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t+2=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2t.$ Substituting into the third equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+2 & =2\\cdot (2t) \\\\ 2t+2 & =4t \\\\ 2t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the second equation ${t}^{3}+5=3c{t}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$ These values also satisfy the second equation. \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\sqrt{1}\\,,\\,{1}^{3}+5\\,,\\,2(1)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,6,\\,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,6,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-165046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,5,2)"] ["math" "(1,6,4)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},13,6)"] ["text" "$(0,5,2)$ and $(\\sqrt{3},32,8)$"] ["text" "$(1,6,4)$ and $(\\sqrt{2},13,6)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,6,4)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{5}-8,2{t}^{4}-6⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨c,\\frac{4c}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{4}=c \\\\ 8{t}^{3}=\\frac{4c}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=5{t}^{4}.$ Substituting $c=5{t}^{4}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{t}^{3} & =\\frac{4(5{t}^{4})}{5} \\\\ 8{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}(2-t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=2.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨(2)^{5}-8,2(2)^{4}-6⟩=⟨24,26⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(24,26).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0556f4231178acbf98d5a5a68ef76c593c42aaaedf71e8d6a11c56d7a7fe16db"} {"question" "q-165038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-7,-4)"] ["math" "(-9,-4)"] ["math" "(24,26)"] ["text" "$(-7,-4)$ and $(24,26)$"] ["text" "$(-9,-4)$ and $(-8,-6)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "q-165038" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(24,26)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "eaaa65cf2fdaefbe32f35d18a17e2ed6ec2167e4d0c886cccae52b8787aed272"} {"question" "e-12567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12567.json" "file_sha256" "a4e0f7afc54eedd8b18b64741f483749958ec3f0efc37e78363e54f6c281daec"} {"question" "e-12567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12567.json" "file_sha256" "a4e0f7afc54eedd8b18b64741f483749958ec3f0efc37e78363e54f6c281daec"} {"question" "e-12568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12568.json" "file_sha256" "847c584ec03c3122192fcd170e3728c29c404d293f654ecc6dd141379adf1cf6"} {"question" "e-12568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12568.json" "file_sha256" "847c584ec03c3122192fcd170e3728c29c404d293f654ecc6dd141379adf1cf6"} {"question" "e-12580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12580.json" "file_sha256" "4eef03666effb06cc7ab81ab916550691d098899a1682df4b04f56d030b7dd88"} {"question" "e-12580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 6t+6 & =0 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}+1\\,,\\,6(-1)+3⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12580.json" "file_sha256" "4eef03666effb06cc7ab81ab916550691d098899a1682df4b04f56d030b7dd88"} {"question" "e-12569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12569.json" "file_sha256" "86b1cc6461fc7afc37bc987047b25162e2c937795160255649aec7f8cb8483ed"} {"question" "e-12569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12569.json" "file_sha256" "86b1cc6461fc7afc37bc987047b25162e2c937795160255649aec7f8cb8483ed"}] "usage" {"q-33498" [] "e-12568" [] "q-32916" [] "e-12580" [] "e-12567" [] "e-12569" []} "basis" 1723 "retractions" [[17592186120145 :question/problem "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t) = ⟨2(t - \\sin t), 2(t - \\cos t)⟩$ at the point where $t = π$."] [17592186120186 :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t) = ti + (1 + \\frac{1}{2}t^{2})j$, find all of the points at which $r^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular."] [17592186119436 :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1)$."] [17592186119436 :question/worked-solution "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t$. At this point, the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P$. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= i + j + k \\\\\nti + t^{2}j + t^{3}k &= i + j + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j$, and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n{t = 1; t^{2} = 1; t^{3} = 1\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1$. This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1)$.\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3})k \\\\\n&= i + 2tj + 3t^{2}k \\\\\nr^{′}(1) &= i + 2(1)j + 3(1)^{2}k \\\\\n&= i + 2j + 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1792/Source/Images/1d982638f3bd912bc8329318369e03d5.png)"] [17592186119437 :question/problem "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$."] [17592186119437 :question/worked-solution "Let $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nAt this point, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}), \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}), 4(\\frac{π}{2})^{2}⟩ = ⟨0, 1, π^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(4t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\sin t, \\cos t, 8t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨ - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}), \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}), 8(\\frac{π}{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 0, 4π⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nR(u) = ⟨0, 1, π^{2}⟩ + u⟨ - 1, 0, 4π⟩, u ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1792/Source/Images/44a11a13457036bfe5aed9e5772179be.png)"] [17592186119439 :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle$, find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$."] [17592186119439 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} + 1), \\frac{d}{dt}(6t + 3)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2t, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨3, 1⟩ \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨3, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨2t, 6⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 2t + 1 \\cdot 6 &= 0 \\\\\n6t + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(-1) &= ⟨(-1)^{2} + 1, 6(-1) + 3⟩ = ⟨2,-3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3)$."] [17592186119438 :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$."] [17592186119438 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} + t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t)j \\\\\n&= (2t + 1)i + (2t - 1)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n(2t + 1)i + (2t - 1)j = c(3i + j)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{2t + 1 = 3c; 2t - 1 = c\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1$. Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t + 1 &= 3c \\\\\n2t + 1 &= 3(2t - 1) \\\\\n2t + 1 &= 6t - 3 \\\\\n6t - 2t &= 1 + 3 \\\\\n4t &= 4 \\\\\nt &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(1) &= (1^{2} + 1)i + (1^{2} - 1)j = 2i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1792/Source/Images/f28e49053aee4f84b3dff0ab2a0ec87d.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186117391 17592186117392 17592186117393 17592186117394 17592186117395 17592186117637 17592186117638 17592186117639 17592186117640 17592186117641] "immutable_fields" [[17592186117390 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117390 :answer-field/id] [17592186117390 :answer-field/key] [17592186117390 :answer-field/type] [17592186117636 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117636 :answer-field/id] [17592186117636 :answer-field/key] [17592186117636 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
